<section class="l-section">
  <div class="l-container">
    <p class="hero__kicker">Stack and decisions</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">How this site is built</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">
      No framework, no bundler, no minification. Not a stance against tools, but a test
      of how much can be done with less.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="l-section">
  <div class="l-container case__body case__body--flush">
    <section class="case__section">
      <h2 class="case__section-title">What it is made of</h2>
      <dl class="decision__facts">
        <dt>Templates</dt>
        <dd>11ty 3 — HTML assembly only. The output is static files, no server.</dd>
        <dt>Styles</dt>
        <dd>One <code>styles.css</code>: <code>@layer</code>, custom properties, BEM. No preprocessor.</dd>
        <dt>Scripts</dt>
        <dd>One <code>main.js</code>, 5.9 KB raw — theme, palette, slider, anatomy. Theme handling and the few things that need JS.</dd>
        <dt>Fonts</dt>
        <dd>Literata for headings, Inter for text. Latin and Cyrillic subsets, 100 KB in total.</dd>
        <dt>Hosting</dt>
        <dd>GitHub Pages. A push to main builds and publishes the site.</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="case__section">
      <h2 class="case__section-title">Why no framework</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The site needs no reactivity, no virtual DOM and no client-side routing: it is a
        set of text pages. A framework here would add tens of kilobytes of JavaScript to
        a page that opens in fractions of a second.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        Frameworks stay where they pay off — in production projects. Here I tested the
        opposite hypothesis: can a quality interface be built on the platform as it is.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case__section">
      <h2 class="case__section-title">Budgets</h2>
      <dl class="metric-list">
        <div class="metric">
          <dt class="metric__label">Page HTML</dt>
          <dd class="metric__value"><span class="metric__after">2.4 KB</span></dd>
          <dd class="metric__method">gzip, budget 6 KB</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="metric">
          <dt class="metric__label">CSS</dt>
          <dd class="metric__value"><span class="metric__after">4.5 KB</span></dd>
          <dd class="metric__method">gzip, budget 8 KB</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="metric">
          <dt class="metric__label">JS on first render</dt>
          <dd class="metric__value"><span class="metric__after">0 KB</span></dd>
          <dd class="metric__method">scripts are deferred, none blocks rendering</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="case__section">
      <h2 class="case__section-title">Accessibility</h2>
      <ul class="case__list">
        <li class="case__list-item">Semantic markup: <code>main</code>, labelled <code>section</code> elements, a labelled <code>nav</code>, <code>article</code> for case studies.</li>
        <li class="case__list-item">Skip link and a visible focus ring on every interactive element.</li>
        <li class="case__list-item">Text contrast at or above 4.5:1 in both themes.</li>
        <li class="case__list-item">Animations turn off under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.</li>
        <li class="case__list-item">The theme follows the system setting until the user picks one.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="case__section">
      <h2 class="case__section-title">What is missing</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Screenshots in the case studies are still placeholders — the real ones will come
        later. A notes section was deliberately left out: an empty blog is worse than none.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>
